Introduction
Many Expert Advisors implement breakeven stops, trailing stops, ATR-based stops, and time exits as independent pieces of logic. Implementing each rule is rarely the difficult part. The difficult part is deciding what happens when several rules trigger on the same tick. A trailing stop might want to move the stop closer, a breakeven rule might want to move it to entry, and a time exit might want to close the position entirely. When every module modifies the position directly, the order in which the modules run decides the outcome, and that order is often implicit and undocumented.
This article presents an architecture where exit modules never modify a position directly. Each module produces a proposal. A policy engine ranks all well-formed proposals in a strict, documented order. A validator checks the candidates in that order against risk invariants and broker constraints, and the first candidate that passes is handed to an execution adapter, the only component that interacts with the trade server. A reconciliation step then compares the broker-side position with the request before the internal state is updated. The engine does not decide when to enter. It only manages positions after they are open, using an R-multiple framework so that every threshold is expressed relative to the initial risk of the position.
The goal is to demonstrate a modular, broker-aware, deterministic decision process that can be tested and explained. The result is a seven-stage pipeline: Proposal, Ranking, Validation (with fallback to the next candidate), Execution, Reconciliation, State update, and Trace.
Why Traditional Exit Logic Becomes Difficult
Imagine a BUY position that reaches several conditions at once. The configuration uses a breakeven threshold of 0.5R, a trailing activation of 1.5R, and a maximum lifetime of 120 minutes:
Price has advanced to +2.0R, so both the breakeven threshold and the trailing activation are reached.
The position has been open for 120 minutes, so the time exit condition is satisfied.
Now three modules want different things:
Breakeven: move SL to entry plus buffer.
Trailing: move SL to current price minus a fixed distance.
Time exit: close the position completely.
Which one should win? This is a strategy decision, not a technical one. One strategy treats the maximum lifetime as a hard rule; another prefers to secure breakeven first and let the trailing stop work. If each module modifies the position on its own, the answer is decided by whichever module runs last, which is fragile and hard to reproduce.
The architecture proposed here turns each module into a proposal generator. Each generator returns an SExitAdjustment structure that describes what it would like to do but does not touch the broker. The policy engine ranks all proposals by a priority that comes from configuration, and the validator decides, candidate by candidate, which request the broker can accept. The selection rule is therefore visible in configuration and testable, instead of being hidden in the order of function calls.
Figure 1: Exit pipeline with ranked proposals, validation fallback, reconciliation and decision trace.
Shared Data Model
The engine is built around a few core structs and enums defined in AdaptiveExitTypes.mqh. A position can be in several management states at the same time, for example after a partial close while the trailing stop is active. The state is therefore a bit mask of ENUM_POSITION_FLAG values instead of a single enum value:
// --- Management flags of a position (bit mask, flags can be combined)
enum
ENUM_POSITION_FLAG
  {
   PSF_INITIAL_STOP  =
1
,
// Confirmed protective stop exists and 1R is measured
PSF_BREAKEVEN     =
2
,
// Breakeven stop move confirmed by the broker
PSF_TRAILING      =
4
,
// At least one trailing stop move confirmed
PSF_PARTIAL_TAKEN =
8
,
// Partial close filled (fully or partly)
PSF_CLOSED        =
16
// Full close confirmed
};
The SPositionSnapshot holds live data captured from the terminal, plus the management fields copied from the persistent state:
// --- Snapshot of a position at a given moment (transient live data)
struct
SPositionSnapshot
  {
ulong
ticket;
// Unique position ticket
string
symbol;
// Trading symbol (e.g., EURUSD)
long
magic;
// Expert Advisor magic number
ENUM_POSITION_TYPE
direction;
// POSITION_TYPE_BUY or SELL
double
entry_price;
// Open price (price units)
double
current_price;
// Bid for long, ask for short (price units)
double
current_sl;
// Current stop-loss (price units, 0 if none)
double
current_tp;
// Current take-profit (price units, 0 if none)
double
volume;
// Position volume (lots)
datetime
open_time;
// Open time (timestamp)
double
initial_risk_distance;
// 1R distance |entry - initial SL| (price units)
double
current_R;
// Unrealized profit/loss (R-multiple)
double
max_favorable_R;
// Highest current R reached (R-multiple)
uint
flags;
// ENUM_POSITION_FLAG bits from management state
};
Two fields need a precise definition. The field initial_risk_distance is 1R expressed as an absolute price distance, |entry - initial SL|, in price units (not points). The field current_R stores the unrealized result of the open position in R-multiples. A realized result exists only after a full or partial close. Both values are 0 until a protective stop has been confirmed and 1R is known.
The SManagementState is separate from the snapshot. It persists 1R, the maximum favorable R, and the flags across ticks. Without this persistence the engine would lose the original risk distance every time it queries the terminal. The pending fields hold a request that the broker has accepted but whose result is not yet visible on the position (see State Reconciliation):
// --- Persistent management state for a position (stored separately)
struct
SManagementState
  {
ulong
ticket;
// Position ticket (key for lookup)
double
initial_risk_distance;
// 1R from a confirmed loss-side stop (price units)
double
max_favorable_R;
// Highest R reached (R-multiple)
uint
flags;
// ENUM_POSITION_FLAG bits, source of truth for state
datetime
last_update_time;
// Last state update (timestamp)
bool
pending;
// True while a request waits for broker confirmation
ENUM_EXIT_ACTION  pending_action;
// Action of the outstanding request
ENUM_EXIT_REASON  pending_reason;
// Reason of the outstanding request
double
pending_sl;
// Requested stop-loss (price units)
double
pending_close_volume;
// Requested close volume (lots)
double
pending_base_volume;
// Position volume when the request was sent (lots)
datetime
pending_since;
// Time the request was sent (timestamp)
};
SExitAdjustment represents a proposal from a module:
// --- Proposal generated by an exit module (ranked later by the policy engine)
struct
SExitAdjustment
  {
ulong
ticket;
// Position ticket to which this proposal applies
ENUM_EXIT_ACTION  action;
// Action to perform
double
proposed_sl;
// New stop-loss for STOP_MODIFICATION (price units)
double
proposed_tp;
// Take-profit to keep for STOP_MODIFICATION (price units)
double
close_volume;
// Volume to close for PARTIAL/FULL_EXIT (lots)
ENUM_EXIT_REASON  reason;
// Why this proposal was generated
string
explanation;
// Human-readable description of the proposal logic
bool
well_formed;
// Module-level sanity only, not broker validity
};
The action type is separate from the reason. A time exit is a FULL_EXIT action with a TIME reason. An ATR stop is a STOP_MODIFICATION action with an ATR reason. The policy engine uses both, as described in the next sections.
The field well_formed only states that the module produced a sane proposal (fields filled, a positive price or volume). It does not mean that the broker will accept the proposal. That decision belongs exclusively to the validator, which returns an ENUM_EXIT_RESULT. The field close_volume is the volume to close in lots; it is always 0 for a stop modification.
Proposal Generation
Each exit module implements a GenerateProposal method. It receives an SPositionSnapshot and fills an SExitAdjustment. It never calls OrderSend. This keeps broker-side execution outside the proposal modules and makes them testable with synthetic snapshots. Each module sets its own action; the engine never rewrites it.
The ATR calculator creates the indicator handle once in the constructor, caches it, and releases it in the destructor. It reads ATR from the last closed bar (shift 1), so the value does not change within a bar:
// --- Read ATR of the last closed bar, which does not change within the bar
bool
GetClosedBarATR(
double
&value, ENUM_EXIT_ERROR &error)
     {
if
(m_atr_handle ==
INVALID_HANDLE
)
// If handle is not valid
{
         error = EXIT_INVALID_CONFIG;
// Set configuration error
return
false
;
// Return failure
}
double
atr[
1
];
// Buffer for one ATR value
if
(
CopyBuffer
(m_atr_handle,
0
,
1
,
1
, atr) !=
1
|| atr[
0
] <=
0
)
// Shift 1 = last closed bar
{
         error = EXIT_NOT_ENOUGH_DATA;
// Set insufficient data error
return
false
;
// Return failure
}
