A return code describes what the trade server answered, not what the position looks like afterwards. The adapter therefore classifies the code instead of treating it as the final result:
// --- Map a return code to an engine result; DONE_PARTIAL and PLACED still need reconciliation
static
ENUM_EXIT_RESULT ClassifyRetcode(
uint
retcode)
     {
switch
(retcode)
// Branch on return code
{
case
TRADE_RETCODE_DONE
:
// Request completed
return
EXIT_RESULT_OK;
// Executed
case
TRADE_RETCODE_DONE_PARTIAL
:
// Only part of the volume filled
return
EXIT_RESULT_PARTIAL_FILL;
// Partly executed
case
TRADE_RETCODE_PLACED
:
// Order placed, not filled yet
return
EXIT_RESULT_PENDING_CONFIRMATION;
// Outcome not visible yet
default
:
// Any other code
return
EXIT_RESULT_EXECUTION_ERROR;
// Failure
}
     }
Only TRADE_RETCODE_DONE counts as executed. TRADE_RETCODE_DONE_PARTIAL and TRADE_RETCODE_PLACED are accepted requests whose effect still has to be confirmed on the position. Every other code is an execution error, and the management state is left unchanged. Execute() returns an SExitResult whose success flag states whether the request was accepted; the final values it reports are expected values until reconciliation confirms them.
State Reconciliation and Explainability
After the execution adapter returns, the engine reads the position again and compares it with the request. A stop modification is confirmed when the broker-side SL is within half a tick of the requested level. A partial close is confirmed when the volume has dropped by the requested amount; a smaller drop counts as a partial fill. A full close is confirmed when the position no longer exists. If the result is not visible yet, for example after TRADE_RETCODE_PLACED, the request is stored as pending. On subsequent ticks, the pending request is checked first, and no new request is sent for that position until the result appears or pending_timeout_seconds expires.
