The tests are named "Deterministic and Broker-Aware Module Tests" because some depend on live symbol data while others use only synthetic inputs. This distinction matters for an honest evaluation: a B test that passes on one symbol is evidence for that symbol, not proof for all symbols.
Scope of the Engine
The engine intentionally does not:
Generate trade entries (the optional demo entry exists only to give the engine a position to manage).
Predict market direction.
Determine whether a strategy has positive expectancy.
Perform portfolio optimization or coordinate decisions across positions.
Guarantee execution at the requested price.
Guarantee that a close request results in a closed position; reconciliation checks the outcome instead of assuming it.
Guarantee profitability.
It receives an already-open position and determines how that position should be managed. Keeping this scope narrow keeps each stage of the pipeline small enough to test on its own.
Known Limitations and Future Work
No research prototype is complete. The following limitations are known and should be considered in any deployment:
Management state is memory-resident. A terminal restart loses 1R, the maximum favorable R, the flags, and any pending request.
The filling mode is chosen from SYMBOL_FILLING_MODE in the order FOK, IOC, RETURN. RETURN is a fallback and can be rejected on symbols with market execution.
The initial ATR stop is measured from entry. If price has already moved beyond that level, the stop is rejected. If no structural stop passes either, the position stays without a protective stop, and the trace reports this on every tick until a candidate passes.
After pending_timeout_seconds an unconfirmed request is re-evaluated. With a very slow broker this can lead to a second request for the same action.
Persistence is synchronous CSV logging, which is transparent but not high-throughput. ExplainDecision() keeps only the last trace per position; the full history exists only in the CSV log.
Slippage, latency, and execution quality are not modeled.
The structure detector is a configurable pivot model, not a universal market structure engine.
The state and trace tables have a fixed capacity of 100 positions.
Tests marked B depend on live symbol data and are not fully deterministic across symbols and brokers. ATR is read from the last closed bar, so it is reproducible within a bar, not across different data feeds.
Each position is processed independently, and at most one action is executed per position per tick. Strategies that need several simultaneous actions, conditional order sequences, or portfolio-level coordination are outside the current scope.
The partial exit triggers on the maximum favorable R, so it can still fire after price has retraced below the trigger level.
These limitations do not diminish the core contribution: a pipeline that separates proposals from execution, validates candidates against broker rules in a documented order, confirms outcomes before changing state, and records each decision.
Conclusion
The key design point is the separation between the actions that modules propose and the actions that the broker accepts. Proposals are ranked by a documented rule, validated candidate by candidate, executed by a single adapter, and confirmed by reconciliation before the internal state changes. This separation makes each stage observable and testable in isolation, and it moves the selection rule out of the order of function calls and into configuration.
The prototype presented here is not a system that guarantees profits. The separation does not by itself make a strategy profitable or its execution reliable: the quality of the decisions still depends on the configured priorities, on the individual exit rules, and on the broker. What the pipeline provides is a decision process that can be inspected. Every tick leaves a trace that shows what was proposed, what was rejected and why, what was sent, and what the broker-side position looked like afterwards.
New exit rules can be added as proposal generators, each with its own reason and priority slot, without rewriting the engine. Strategies that require several simultaneous actions per tick or coordination across positions need extensions beyond the design shown here.
